1
00:00:00,125 --> 00:00:05,649
(Q31) So, welcome to your introductory geography lecture. We'll begin with some basics.

2
00:00:05,649 --> 00:00:09,824
Firstly, what do we learn by studying geography?

3
00:00:09,824 --> 00:00:17,599
[Q31答案句] Well, we learn a great deal about all the processes that have affected and that continue to affect the earth's surface.

4
00:00:17,599 --> 00:00:29,990
(Q32) [Q32答案句] But we learn far more than that, because studying geography also informs us about the different kinds of relationships that develop between a particular environment and the people that live there.

5
00:00:29,990 --> 00:00:35,224
(Q33) Okay. We like to think of geography as having two main branches.

6
00:00:35,224 --> 00:00:48,003
[Q33答案句] There's the study of the nature of our planet - its physical features, what it actually looks like - and then there's the study of the ways in which we choose to live and of the impact of those on our planet.

7
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:52,107
(Q34) Our current use of carbon fuels is a good example of that.

8
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:59,685
But there are more specific study areas to consider too, and we'll be looking at each of these in turn throughout this semester.

9
00:00:59,685 --> 00:01:07,573
These include bio-physical geography, by which I mean the study of the natural environment and all its living things.

10
00:01:07,573 --> 00:01:12,653
Then there's topography - that looks at the shapes of the land and oceans.

11
00:01:12,653 --> 00:01:21,043
There's the study of political geography and social geography too, of course, which is the study of communities of people.

12
00:01:21,043 --> 00:01:28,701
We have economic geography - in which we examine all kinds of resources and their use - agriculture, for example.

13
00:01:28,701 --> 00:01:39,888
Next comes historical geography - the understanding of how people and their environments and the ways they interact have changed over a period of time -

14
00:01:39,888 --> 00:01:54,538
[Q34答案句] and urban geography, an aspect I'm particularly interested in, which takes as its focus the location of cities, the services that those cities provide, and migration of people to and from such cities.

15
00:01:54,538 --> 00:01:57,673
(Q35) And lastly, we have cartography.

16
00:01:57,673 --> 00:02:03,164
That's the art and science of map-making. You'll be doing a lot of that!

17
00:02:03,164 --> 00:02:09,829
So, to summarise before we continue, we now have our key answer...

18
00:02:09,829 --> 00:02:21,920
[Q35答案句] Studying this subject is important because without geographical knowledge, we would know very little about our surroundings and we wouldn't be able to identify all the problems that relate to them.

19
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:28,520
(Q36) So, by definition, we wouldn't be in an informed position to work out how to solve any of them.

20
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:34,880
Okay, now for some practicalities. What do geographers actually do?

21
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,931
Well, we collect data to begin with!

22
00:02:37,931 --> 00:02:41,113
You'll be doing a lot of that on your first field trip!

23
00:02:41,113 --> 00:02:44,178
How do we do this? There are several means.

24
00:02:44,178 --> 00:02:50,663
We might, for example, conduct a census - count a population in a given area perhaps.

25
00:02:50,663 --> 00:03:00,061
[Q36答案句] We also need images of the earth's surface which we can produce by means of computer-generation technology or with the help of satellite relays.

26
00:03:00,061 --> 00:03:10,308
(Q37) We've come a very long way from the early exploration of the world by sailing ships when geographers only had pens and paper at their disposal.

27
00:03:10,308 --> 00:03:14,485
After we've gathered our information, we must analyse it!

28
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:20,324
[Q37答案句] We need to look for patterns, most commonly those of causes and consequences.

29
00:03:20,324 --> 00:03:26,618
(Q38) This kind of information helps us to predict and resolve problems that could affect the world we live in.

30
00:03:26,618 --> 00:03:30,449
But we don't keep all this information confidential.

31
00:03:30,449 --> 00:03:36,631
We then need to publish our findings so that other people can access it and be informed by it.

32
00:03:36,631 --> 00:03:42,113
And one way in which this information can be published is in the form of maps.

33
00:03:42,113 --> 00:03:46,197
You'll all have used one at some stage of your life already.

34
00:03:46,197 --> 00:03:50,642
Let's consider the benefits of maps from a geographer's perspective.

35
00:03:50,642 --> 00:03:58,284
Maps can be folded and put in a pocket and can provide a great store of reference when they're collected into an atlas.

36
00:03:58,284 --> 00:04:06,780
They can depict the physical features of the entire planet if necessary, or, just a small part of it in much greater detail.

37
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,627
But there is a drawback.

38
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:22,547
[Q38答案句] You can't exactly replicate something that is three-dimensional, like our planet, on a flat piece of paper, because paper has only two dimensions, and that means there'll always be a certain degree of distortion on a map.

39
00:04:22,547 --> 00:04:24,827
(Q39) It can't be avoided.

40
00:04:24,827 --> 00:04:32,134
We can also use aerial photographs, pictures taken by cameras at high altitude above the earth.

41
00:04:32,134 --> 00:04:38,695
These are great for showing all kinds of geographical features that are not easy to see from the ground.

42
00:04:38,695 --> 00:04:49,671
[Q39答案句] You can easily illustrate areas of diseased trees or how much traffic is on the roads at a given time or information about deep sea beds, for example.

43
00:04:49,671 --> 00:04:59,135
(Q40) Then there are Landsats. These are satellites that circle the earth and transmit visual information to computers at receiving stations.

44
00:04:59,135 --> 00:05:09,339
[Q40答案句] They circle the earth several times a day and can provide a mass of information - you'll all be familiar with the information they give us about the weather, for example.

45
00:05:09,339 --> 00:05:11,839
So, what we're going to do now is look at a short presentation in which you'll see all these tools...

